# GENERATED FILES. Formatting a generated file is a guaranteed drift bug: the
# generator keeps emitting its own layout, the formatter keeps rewriting it, and
# the byte-identical guard between them fails forever. Prettier's own docs give
# exactly this reason for .prettierignore -- so `prettier --write .` does not
# choke on or mangle generated output -- and 2026 guidance singles lockfiles out
# because a rewrite breaks install determinism.
#
# Each entry below is REGENERATED by a registered op, and each has a guard
# asserting the file matches a fresh render:
#   pnpm-lock.yaml                     <- pnpm install
#   .sops.yaml                         <- //#env:recipients   (env-bootstrap-roster.guard)
#   .secrets.baseline                  <- //#secrets:baseline
#   design-token-ratchet.tsv           <- tokens:lint --tighten
#
# .sops.yaml is the one that actually bit: the burndown reformatted its age
# recipient block from 9-space to 7-space continuation indent, and
# env-bootstrap-roster.guard.test.ts -- which asserts the file is BYTE-IDENTICAL
# to renderSopsConfig(roster) -- failed immediately. That guard is correct and
# load-bearing (a .sops.yaml that drifts from .age-recipients silently locks a
# machine out of decryption), so the formatter is what must yield.
pnpm-lock.yaml
.sops.yaml
.secrets.baseline
apps/ops-web/design-token-ratchet.tsv

# ENCRYPTED SECRET MATERIAL. .env.sops.yaml holds SOPS-encrypted values, and the
# burndown rewrote 32 of its lines before this entry existed. Reformatting
# ciphertext is not cosmetic: sops computes a MAC over the document, every other
# machine bootstraps by decrypting this file, and a formatter has no reason to
# touch bytes no human reads. Restored and excluded.
.env.sops.yaml

# drizzle-kit GENERATES these snapshots by diffing the schema barrel; db:generate
# owns their layout and re-emits it on every migration. Same class as the lockfile.
apps/api/src/database/migrations/meta/

# next-env.d.ts is GENERATED by `next build` and rewritten on every run. It is
# git-ignored (apps/ops-web/.gitignore) and eslint-ignored (eslint.config.mjs),
# and this was the one ignore list that missed it -- so it never appeared on a
# clean CI checkout and surfaced only after a local build, making //#format:check
# fail on a laptop while passing in CI. Next.js states plainly that the file
# should not be edited.
**/next-env.d.ts
